 There needs to be some table used for locking; as we need to ensure multiple brokers don't write to the same logical database. Though by all means suggest a suitable workaround that works for you with database replication; would ignoring the table, or moving it to a separate logical database/schema work? 

 Worst case scenario; we could use a completely separate database entirely (even a different JDBC provider!   but I'd rather reuse the same DataSource if we can (it makes things much simpler to code and removes a multitude of possible issues such as folks using a local Derby DB for the lock
- [marsangr]: Our DBAs confirm that configuring the replication server to ignore the lock-table is a possible workaround with our system. This might be manufacturer-dependent. Using a lock table in a different logical database (not to be replicated) is another possible solution. 

